RISC-V: null-check probes narrower than a pointer load zero-extending (lbu/lhu/lwu).

The probe of a GT_NULLCHECK discards its value into x0. ZisK proves lb/lh/lw as a load plus a
sign-extension operation (BinaryExtension) and lbu/lhu/lwu as a plain load; the faulting behaviour
is the same.

Nethermind stateless guest on ZisK: signextend_b 42,325 -> 300 on block 26055159;
cost -2,364,040 / -4,769,128 on blocks 26055159 / 26112793, steps unchanged.
Outputs identical on 11 mainnet blocks.

diff --git a/src/coreclr/jit/codegenriscv64.cpp b/src/coreclr/jit/codegenriscv64.cpp
--- a/src/coreclr/jit/codegenriscv64.cpp
+++ b/src/coreclr/jit/codegenriscv64.cpp
@@ -5178,7 +5178,25 @@
         type = TYP_LONG;
     }
 
-    GetEmitter()->emitInsLoadStoreOp(ins_Load(type), emitActualTypeSize(type), REG_R0, tree);
+    // The probe discards its value, so a narrower one need not sign-extend it: ZisK proves lb/lh/lw as a load
+    // plus a sign-extension operation, and lbu/lhu/lwu as a plain load.
+    instruction ins = ins_Load(type);
+    switch (ins)
+    {
+        case INS_lb:
+            ins = INS_lbu;
+            break;
+        case INS_lh:
+            ins = INS_lhu;
+            break;
+        case INS_lw:
+            ins = INS_lwu;
+            break;
+        default:
+            break;
+    }
+
+    GetEmitter()->emitInsLoadStoreOp(ins, emitActualTypeSize(type), REG_R0, tree);
 }
 
 //------------------------------------------------------------------------
